<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Expedition of the Thousand</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Spedizione dei Mille</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Expedition of the Thousand was the campaign led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> in 1860, in which about a thousand volunteers in red shirts sailed from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsala">Marsala</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in May. They defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> forces, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> and crossed to the mainland, entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in September. The fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a> opened the way for the proclamation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1861.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand.jpg" alt="Expedition of the Thousand" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partenza_da_Quarto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The expedition was an ambitious and risky venture, aiming to conquer with about a thousand men a kingdom with a larger regular army and a more powerful navy. It was the only action jointly decided by the four Fathers of the Fatherland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour</a>, who pursued divergent goals. Mazzini, a republican, wanted to liberate southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Garibaldi wanted to conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a> in the king's name and go on towards Rome. Cavour wanted to avoid taking Rome so as not to offend his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>, who protected the Papal States.</p><p>In 1860 Sicilian independentism was the one force willing to take up arms against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>. A revolt began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on 4 April and was quickly repressed, but it led to further demonstrations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> thought the expedition risky, yet he openly supported it only when its chances of success were significant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In March 1860 the exile Rosolino Pilo urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> to lead an expedition, and by May Garibaldi had gathered 1,089 volunteers. They left Quarto near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> on the night of 5 to 6 May. Garibaldi landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsala">Marsala</a> on 11 May, helped by the presence of two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> ships in the port, which deterred the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> ships from interfering. On 14 May at Salemi he assumed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a>, and about a thousand Sicilian volunteers joined him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on 21 June, and the news drew support abroad. Workers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> gave days of work, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a> brought weapons. On 7 July he proclaimed himself Dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Reinforcements formed the Southern Army. After Milazzo on 20 July he prepared the crossing to the mainland, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilicata">Basilicata</a> on 2 September, and advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> without obstacles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Plebiscites in October 1860 brought the south into the Kingdom of Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> was proclaimed on 17 March 1861. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 9 November 1860, six months and three days after setting out, and returned to Caprera. Spain and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> broke off diplomatic relations with Piedmont-Sardinia.</p><p>Many expectations were disappointed, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, where farmers and the poor faced higher taxes and compulsory conscription. Brigandage later required some 140,000 Piedmontese troops to hold the former kingdom. Some historians trace the Southern Question to the enterprise.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Expedition of the Thousand” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That May Giuseppe Garibaldi’s <b>Expedition of the Thousand</b> landed in Sicily and overthrew the Bourbons in the south, and Sardinian troops marched through the Papal Marche and Umbria to meet him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state lasted until Garibaldi's <b>Expedition of the Thousand</b> in 1860 and its annexation to the Kingdom of Italy in 1861.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cavour’s diplomacy, from the Crimean War to the agreement with Napoleon III at Plombières in 1858, drew France into war with Austria and allowed the kingdom to absorb Lombardy, the central Italian duchies and, after Giuseppe Garibaldi’s <b>Expedition of the Thousand</b>, the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When Giuseppe Garibaldi landed in Sicily in 1860 at the head of the <b>Expedition of the Thousand</b>, with the connivance of Cavour, many rallied to him; he crossed to the mainland and won the Battle of the Volturno with half his army local volunteers, and the Bourbon army…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The title once carried honour: Giuseppe Garibaldi officially styled himself “Dictator” of Sicily during the <b>Expedition of the Thousand</b> in 1860, and leaders of Poland’s November Uprising of 1830 and January Uprising of 1863 held the same title.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Garibaldi's volunteers sang it in the <b>Expedition of the Thousand</b> in 1860, and Giuseppe Verdi chose it to stand for Italy in his &quot;Hymn of the Nations&quot; for the London International Exhibition of 1862.</blockquote><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani</a></li></ul></section>
